3. ENTERPRISE USE AND COMMERCIAL USE

"Enterprise" means a for-profit business or organization of any size,
including a company, partnership, sole proprietorship, startup, affiliate,
or other entity carrying on business for commercial advantage.

"Enterprise Use" means use of the Material by an Enterprise, or work using
the Material on behalf of, at the direction of, under contract to, or for the
business benefit of an Enterprise. This includes internal research,
benchmarking, evaluation, employee or contractor work, development, testing,
training, integration, deployment, and production. No revenue, distribution,
external customer, minimum company size, or production launch is required.

An individual employed by an Enterprise may use the free grant for genuinely
personal, independent noncommercial research that is not work for or for the
business benefit of that Enterprise. Employment alone does not prohibit
personal study. Enterprise-directed or enterprise-sponsored work is not
personal study merely because it is performed outside normal working hours.

"Commercial Use" means use of the Material in connection with a product,
service, paid application, consulting engagement, commercial offering, or
other activity undertaken for commercial advantage or monetary compensation,
whether conducted by an Enterprise, an individual, or a nonprofit entity.
An ordinary salary, student stipend, or public or nonprofit research grant
does not by itself make otherwise eligible noncommercial teaching or academic
research Commercial Use. This clarification does not exempt any Enterprise
Use, including enterprise-sponsored or enterprise-directed work.
Research funded or commissioned for an Enterprise's business purposes is
Enterprise Use even when an academic institution performs it.

